19. Large files
    19.1 A file is large if it is over 20,000 bytes, or if a previous push of that file was truncated. Today that includes gremlin/ui/device.py (~60k) and any later file of that size.
    19.2 Do not send a large file through create_or_update_file or push_files. Those tools require the whole body in the tool call and have truncated device.py to PLACEHOLDER or ~2k bytes.
    19.3 For a large-file change, ask the user for a session Personal Access Token and give the create / copy commands below. Use that token only in this chat, via Python requests against the GitHub API: fetch the last good copy, apply a hunk, PUT the full patched file, then verify size.
         Create a fine-grained token (browser):
           GitHub → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Fine-grained tokens → Generate new token.
           Resource owner: swill008. Repository access: only JoystickGremlin_. Permissions: Contents Read and write. Short expiry.
         Or copy a GitHub CLI token already on the PC:
           gh auth status
           gh auth token
         Paste the token in chat for this session only. Do not commit it. Do not put it in this file.
    19.4 If the user refuses a session token, use this fallback:
         19.4.1 Edit by hunk only: exact old text and exact new text. Copy / replace / paste. Do not rewrite or move the file.
         19.4.2 The user applies the hunk locally (GitHub Desktop / git) and pushes. Grok then inspects size and key symbols on the branch.
    19.5 Never store a token in grok_rules, the repo, artifacts, or project memory. Session tokens die with the chat. Prefer revoke after use.
    19.6 After any write to a large file, confirm: byte size, not PLACEHOLDER, and required symbols (for device.py: class Device, InputIdentifier, map-to-vjoy, and map-to-xbox when that token is expected). Committed size must match the patched size before saying it landed.
    19.7 If a large file is truncated, restore from the last known good commit first (device.py: e592376, 60033 bytes) then re-apply only the missing hunk. Do not layer patches on a stub.
    19.8 Small files stay on the GitHub connector as today.
20. EVO R Button Map lock lives in qml/vkb_evo_r_face_map.md. Read that file before moving any face leader or plus. Update that file in the same commit as any hotspot change.
21. Face leaders — precision only. No guessing. No window-screenshot math.
    21.1 Root cause of missed leaders: (a) nx/ny were read off a GUI screenshot that includes gutters and chrome, but px/py are fractions of the painted JPEG only; (b) tag ends used mapToItem(_face) while photo ends used _stage.x, which is not face space. Do not repeat either.
    21.2 Measure on the source bitmap only. For EVO R that is qml/images/vkb_gladiator_rig.jpg (899 x 920). Pixel x,y of the control, then nx = x / width, ny = y / height.
    21.3 Prove the dots on a copy of that JPEG and show it in chat before changing QML. The user confirms the overlay, not a window screenshot.
    21.4 Write the confirmed row into qml/vkb_evo_r_face_map.md and the matching stroke() in the same commit.
    21.5 Both ends of every leader must use the same item space:
         photoPt(nx, ny) = _img.mapToItem(_face, _ox + nx * _pw, _oy + ny * _ph)
         tagPt = item.mapToItem(_face, …)
         Never use _stage.x / _stage.y as face coordinates.
    21.6 If a dot is still off, change one pixel pair on the JPEG overlay, then one map row. Do not nudge from a window screenshot.
